        "title": "kernel: isofs: validate Rock Ridge CE continuation extent against volume size"
      },
      {
        "cve": "CVE-2026-64530",
        "cwe": {
          "id": "CWE-431",
          "name": "Missing Handler"
        },
        "discovery_date": "2026-07-21T19:12:58.596000+00:00",
        "ids": [
          {
            "system_name": "Red Hat Bugzilla ID",
            "text": "2504052"
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "category": "description",
            "text": "A flaw was found in the Linux kernel's traffic control (TC) classifier application programming interface (API). The `tcf_qevent_handle` function does not properly handle a consumed socket buffer (`skb`) when it is processed by the defragmentation engine. This can lead to a Use-After-Free (UAF) vulnerability, where the system attempts to use memory that has already been freed. An attacker could potentially exploit this to cause system instability or execute arbitrary code.",
            "title": "Vulnerability description"
          },
          {
            "category": "summary",
            "text": "kernel: net: sched: UAF via missing handler for TC_ACT_CONSUMED in tcf_qevent_handle",
            "title": "Vulnerability summary"
          },
          {
            "category": "other",
            "text": "This Important flaw in the Linux kernel's networking scheduler can lead to a use-after-free vulnerability. Exploitation requires a local attacker to configure specific `tc qdisc` and `tc filter` rules involving RED qdisc with early drop events and connection tracking on fragmented network traffic. This non-default configuration limits the attack surface, but successful exploitation could lead to privilege escalation or a denial of service.",
            "title": "Statement"
          },
          {
            "category": "general",
            "text": "The CVSS score(s) listed for this vulnerability do not reflect the associated product's status, and are included for informational purposes to better understand the severity of this vulnerability.",
            "title": "CVSS score applicability"
          }
        ],
